{"task_id": "HumanEval/96", "solution": "```python\ndef count_up_to(n):\n    \"\"\"Implement a function that takes a non-negative integer and returns an array of the first n\n    integers that are prime numbers and less than n.\n    for example:\n    count_up_to(5) => [2,3]\n    count_up_to(11) => [2,3,5,7]\n    count_up_to(0) => []\n    count_up_to(20) => [2,3,5,7,11,13,17,19]\n    count_up_to(1) => []\n    count_up_to(18) => [2,3,5,7,11,13,17]\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def is_prime(num: int) -> bool:\n        if num < 2:\n            return False\n        for i in range(2, int(num**0.5) + 1):\n            if num % i == 0:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    primes = []\n    for num in range(2, n):\n        if is_prime(num):\n            primes.append(num)\n    return primes\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/91", "solution": "import re\n\ndef is_bored(S):\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the number of boredoms (sentences starting with \"I\") in a string.\n    Sentences are delimited by '.', '?' or '!'.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not S.strip():\n        return 0\n\n    boredom_count = 0\n\n    # Define constants directly within the function as per instructions.\n    SENTENCE_DELIMITERS = ['.', '?', '!']\n    TARGET_WORD = \"I\"\n\n    # Create a regex pattern to split by one or more occurrences of any delimiter.\n    # re.escape is used to ensure special regex characters in delimiters are treated literally.\n    delimiter_pattern = r'[' + re.escape(''.join(SENTENCE_DELIMITERS)) + r']+'\n\n    # Split the input string into potential sentence parts.\n    # This might result in empty strings if delimiters are consecutive or at the start/end.\n    raw_sentences = re.split(delimiter_pattern, S)\n\n    sentences = []\n    for part in raw_sentences:\n        stripped_part = part.strip()\n        if stripped_part: # Only include non-empty, non-whitespace sentences.\n            sentences.append(stripped_part)\n\n    # Iterate through the cleaned sentences and count those that start with \"I\".\n    for sentence in sentences:\n        words = sentence.split() # Split the sentence into words by whitespace.\n\n        # Check if the first word exists and is the target word \"I\" (case-insensitive).\n        if words and words[0].lower() == TARGET_WORD.lower():\n            boredom_count += 1\n\n    return boredom_count"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/101", "solution": "```python\nimport re\n\ndef words_string(s):\n    \"\"\"\n    Splits a string of words separated by commas or spaces into a list of words,\n    handling multiple adjacent delimiters and filtering out empty strings.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Split by one or more commas or spaces, then filter out any empty strings\n    words = re.split(r'[, ]+', s)\n    return [word for word in words if word]\n```"}
