{"task_id": "HumanEval/129", "solution": "```python\ndef minPath(grid, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a grid with N rows and N columns (N >= 2) and a positive integer k,\n    each cell of the grid contains a value. Every integer in the range [1, N * N]\n    inclusive appears exactly once on the cells of the grid.\n\n    You have to find the minimum path of length k in the grid. You can start\n    from any cell, and in each step you can move to any of the neighbor cells,\n    in other words, you can go to cells which share an edge with you current\n    cell.\n    Please note that a path of length k means visiting exactly k cells (not\n    necessarily distinct).\n    You CANNOT go off the grid.\n    A path A (of length k) is considered less than a path B (of length k) if\n    after making the ordered lists of the values on the cells that A and B go\n    through (let's call them lst_A and lst_B), lst_A is lexicographically less\n    than lst_B, in other words, there exist an integer index i (1 <= i <= k)\n    such that lst_A[i] < lst_B[i] and for any j (1 <= j < i) we have\n    lst_A[j] = lst_B[j].\n    It is guaranteed that the answer is unique.\n    Return an ordered list of the values on the cells that the minimum path go through.\n\n    Examples:\n\n        Input: grid = [ [1,2,3], [4,5,6], [7,8,9]], k = 3\n        Output: [1, 2, 1]\n\n        Input: grid = [ [5,9,3], [4,1,6], [7,8,2]], k = 1\n        Output: [1]\n    \"\"\"\n    N = len(grid)\n\n    # Initialize min_path_found with a lexicographically \"large\" path.\n    # Max possible value is N*N. A path of k such values + 1 is guaranteed to be larger.\n    max_grid_val = N * N\n    min_path_found = [max_grid_val + 1] * k\n\n    # Directions for 4-way movement (Right, Left, Down, Up)\n    DIRECTIONS = [(0, 1), (0, -1), (1, 0), (-1, 0)]\n\n    def get_value(r, c):\n        return grid[r][c]\n\n    def is_valid_coord(r, c):\n        return 0 <= r < N and 0 <= c < N\n\n    def dfs(current_path_values, current_pos):\n        nonlocal min_path_found\n\n        # Base case: if the current path has reached the desired length k\n        if len(current_path_values) == k:\n            if current_path_values < min_path_found:\n                min_path_found = list(current_path_values)  # Make a copy\n            return\n\n        # Pruning optimization: If the current path prefix is already lexicographically\n        # greater than or equal to the corresponding prefix of the best path found so far,\n        # then this branch cannot lead to a better path.\n        if current_path_values >= min_path_found[:len(current_path_values)]:\n            return\n\n        r, c = current_pos\n        for dr, dc in DIRECTIONS:\n            next_r, next_c = r + dr, c + dc\n            if is_valid_coord(next_r, next_c):\n                next_value = get_value(next_r, next_c)\n                # Recurse: extend the path and explore from the new position\n                dfs(current_path_values + [next_value], (next_r, next_c))\n\n    # Iterate through all possible starting cells in the grid\n    for r_start in range(N):\n        for c_start in range(N):\n            start_value = get_value(r_start, c_start)\n            # Start a Depth-First Search from each cell\n            dfs([start_value], (r_start, c_start))\n\n    return min_path_found\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/132", "solution": "def is_nested(string):\n    '''\n    Checks if the string contains a valid, nested bracket subsequence (contiguous substring)\n    with a maximum nesting depth of 2 or more.\n    '''\n    n = len(string)\n    for i in range(n):\n        balance = 0\n        max_balance = 0\n        for j in range(i, n):\n            char = string[j]\n            if char == '[':\n                balance += 1\n                max_balance = max(max_balance, balance)\n            elif char == ']':\n                balance -= 1\n\n            if balance < 0:\n                # Substring up to this point is invalid, and any longer substring\n                # starting at 'i' will also be invalid. Break and try next 'i'.\n                break\n\n            # Check if the current substring string[i:j+1] is valid and deeply nested\n            if balance == 0 and max_balance >= 2:\n                return True\n    return False"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/136", "solution": "def largest_smallest_integers(lst):\n    '''\n    Finds the largest negative integer and the smallest positive integer in a list.\n    Returns (largest_negative, smallest_positive), using None if none are found.\n    '''\n    max_neg = None\n    min_pos = None\n\n    for num in lst:\n        if num < 0:\n            if max_neg is None or num > max_neg:\n                max_neg = num\n        elif num > 0:\n            if min_pos is None or num < min_pos:\n                min_pos = num\n    return (max_neg, min_pos)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/137", "solution": "```python\ndef compare_one(a, b):\n    \"\"\"\n    Create a function that takes integers, floats, or strings representing\n    real numbers, and returns the larger variable in its given variable type.\n    Return None if the values are equal.\n    \"\"\"\n    def _parse_to_float(value):\n        \"\"\"\n        Converts a value (int, float, or string) to a float for comparison.\n        Handles string representations with ',' or '.' as decimal separators.\n        \"\"\"\n        if isinstance(value, (int, float)):\n            return float(value)\n        elif isinstance(value, str):\n            cleaned_value = value.replace(',', '.')\n            return float(cleaned_value)\n        # For other types, float() will naturally raise a TypeError if not convertible.\n\n    float_a = _parse_to_float(a)\n    float_b = _parse_to_float(b)\n\n    if float_a > float_b:\n        return a\n    elif float_b > float_a:\n        return b\n    else: # float_a == float_b\n        return None\n```"}
