{"task_id": "HumanEval/153", "solution": "```python\ndef Strongest_Extension(class_name, extensions):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the strongest extension for a given class name based on a strength calculation.\n    The strength is calculated as (number of uppercase letters - number of lowercase letters).\n    \"\"\"\n    strongest_ext_name = \"\"\n    max_strength = -float('inf')\n\n    for i, ext_name in enumerate(extensions):\n        cap_count = sum(1 for char in ext_name if 'A' <= char <= 'Z')\n        sm_count = sum(1 for char in ext_name if 'a' <= char <= 'z')\n        current_strength = cap_count - sm_count\n\n        if current_strength > max_strength:\n            max_strength = current_strength\n            strongest_ext_name = ext_name\n        elif current_strength == max_strength:\n            # Tie-breaker: keep the one that came first in the list\n            if i < extensions.index(strongest_ext_name): # This check is implicit in how max_strength is updated\n                pass # The first one already holds the value\n            # Actually, because we iterate in order, the first one encountered with max_strength will\n            # naturally be stored, and subsequent ones with equal strength will not overwrite it.\n            # So, the 'elif' condition already handles the tie-breaking correctly by doing nothing.\n\n    return f\"{class_name}.{strongest_ext_name}\"\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/157", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef right_angle_triangle(a, b, c):\n    # Checks if the sides form a right-angled triangle, considering triangle inequality and float precision.\n    # Returns True if it is a right-angled triangle, False otherwise.\n\n    FLOAT_TOLERANCE = 1e-9\n\n    # Sides must be positive to form a valid triangle.\n    # The triangle inequality check implicitly handles non-positive values by returning False.\n    if not (a + b > c and a + c > b and b + c > a):\n        return False\n\n    a_sq = a ** 2\n    b_sq = b ** 2\n    c_sq = c ** 2\n\n    # Check for Pythagorean theorem with floating-point tolerance\n    if (math.isclose(a_sq + b_sq, c_sq, rel_tol=FLOAT_TOLERANCE) or\n        math.isclose(a_sq + c_sq, b_sq, rel_tol=FLOAT_TOLERANCE) or\n        math.isclose(b_sq + c_sq, a_sq, rel_tol=FLOAT_TOLERANCE)):\n        return True\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/159", "solution": "```python\ndef eat(number, need, remaining):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates total eaten carrots and remaining stock after a rabbit's meal.\n    Returns a list: [total carrots eaten, carrots left in stock].\n    \"\"\"\n    carrots_to_eat_from_stock = min(need, remaining)\n\n    total_eaten_after_meal = number + carrots_to_eat_from_stock\n    carrots_left_in_stock = remaining - carrots_to_eat_from_stock\n\n    return [total_eaten_after_meal, carrots_left_in_stock]\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/160", "solution": "```python\ndef do_algebra(operator, operand):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given two lists operator, and operand. The first list has basic algebra operations, and\n    the second list is a list of integers. Use the two given lists to build the algebric\n    expression and return the evaluation of this expression.\n    \"\"\"\n    from collections import deque\n\n    class InvalidExpressionError(ValueError):\n        \"\"\"Custom exception for invalid algebraic expressions.\"\"\"\n        pass\n\n    VALID_OPERATORS = {'+', '-', '*', '//', '**'}\n\n    OPERATOR_PRECEDENCE = {\n        '**': 3,\n        '*': 2,\n        '//': 2,\n        '+': 1,\n        '-': 1,\n    }\n\n    BINARY_OPERATIONS = {\n        '+': lambda a, b: a + b,\n        '-': lambda a, b: a - b,\n        '*': lambda a, b: a * b,\n        '//': lambda a, b: a // b,\n        '**': lambda a, b: a ** b,\n    }\n\n    # Input validation\n    if not operator or not operand:\n        raise InvalidExpressionError(\"Operator and operand lists cannot be empty.\")\n    if len(operator) != len(operand) - 1:\n        raise InvalidExpressionError(\"Length of operator list must be one less than operand list.\")\n    if len(operand) < 2:\n        raise InvalidExpressionError(\"Operand list must contain at least two operands.\")\n\n    for op_str in operator:\n        if op_str not in VALID_OPERATORS:\n            raise InvalidExpressionError(f\"Invalid operator found: '{op_str}'\")\n\n    for opd in operand:\n        if not isinstance(opd, int) or opd < 0:\n            raise InvalidExpressionError(f\"Operand must be a non-negative integer: '{opd}'\")\n\n    def _apply_operator(op_symbol, val1, val2):\n        if op_symbol == '//' and val2 == 0:\n            raise ZeroDivisionError(\"Cannot perform floor division by zero.\")\n        return BINARY_OPERATIONS[op_symbol](val1, val2)\n\n    output_queue = deque()\n    operator_stack = deque()\n\n    operand_idx = 0\n    for op_str in operator:\n        output_queue.append(operand[operand_idx])\n        operand_idx += 1\n\n        while (operator_stack and\n               OPERATOR_PRECEDENCE[op_str] <= OPERATOR_PRECEDENCE[operator_stack[-1]] and\n               op_str != '**'): # Right-associativity for exponentiation\n            output_queue.append(operator_stack.pop())\n        operator_stack.append(op_str)\n    output_queue.append(operand[operand_idx]) # Add the last operand\n\n    while operator_stack:\n        output_queue.append(operator_stack.pop())\n\n    # Evaluate RPN\n    evaluation_stack = deque()\n    while output_queue:\n        token = output_queue.popleft()\n        if isinstance(token, int):\n            evaluation_stack.append(token)\n        else: # It's an operator\n            val2 = evaluation_stack.pop()\n            val1 = evaluation_stack.pop()\n            result = _apply_operator(token, val1, val2)\n            evaluation_stack.append(result)\n\n    return evaluation_stack.pop()\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/145", "solution": "```python\ndef order_by_points(nums):\n    \"\"\"\n    Write a function which sorts the given list of integers\n    in ascending order according to the sum of their digits.\n    Note: if there are several items with similar sum of their digits,\n    order them based on their index in original list.\n\n    For example:\n    >>> order_by_points([1, 11, -1, -11, -12]) == [-1, -11, 1, -12, 11]\n    >>> order_by_points([]) == []\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(nums, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'nums' must be a list.\")\n\n    def calculate_digit_sum(num: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the sum of the absolute value of digits of an integer.\"\"\"\n        return sum(int(digit) for digit in str(abs(num)))\n\n    decorated_list = []\n    for index, num in enumerate(nums):\n        if not isinstance(num, int):\n            raise ValueError(f\"List element at index {index} is not an integer: {num}\")\n        digit_sum = calculate_digit_sum(num)\n        decorated_list.append((digit_sum, index, num))\n\n    # Python's Timsort is stable, so sorting by (digit_sum, index)\n    # correctly applies the primary and secondary sort criteria.\n    decorated_list.sort()\n\n    return [num for _, _, num in decorated_list]\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/156", "solution": "```python\ndef int_to_mini_roman(number):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer, obtain its roman numeral equivalent as a string,\n    and return it in lowercase.\n    Restrictions: 1 <= num <= 1000\n    \"\"\"\n    roman_map = {\n        1000: 'M', 900: 'CM', 500: 'D', 400: 'CD', 100: 'C',\n        90: 'XC', 50: 'L', 40: 'XL', 10: 'X', 9: 'IX', 5: 'V',\n        4: 'IV', 1: 'I'\n    }\n\n    result = []\n    temp_num = number\n\n    for value, symbol in roman_map.items():\n        while temp_num >= value:\n            result.append(symbol)\n            temp_num -= value\n\n    return \"\".join(result).lower()\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/158", "solution": "```python\ndef find_max(words):\n    \"\"\"Returns the word with the maximum number of unique characters.\n    If multiple strings tie, returns the one that comes first lexicographically.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not words:\n        # Per design context FR5, an empty list raises ValueError.\n        # The prompt says \"Do NOT add isinstance() checks or raise exceptions for edge cases\"\n        # but the natural behavior of min() on an empty list is to raise ValueError,\n        # which aligns with the design's preference for raising ValueError.\n        # Thus, no explicit check `if not words:` is needed here, as min() handles it.\n        pass\n\n    # The key sorts by:\n    # 1. Negative of unique character count (to find maximum count using min())\n    # 2. Word itself (for lexicographical tie-breaking)\n    return min(words, key=lambda word: (-len(set(word)), word))\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/162", "solution": "```python\ndef string_to_md5(text):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a string 'text', return its md5 hash equivalent string.\n    If 'text' is an empty string, return None.\n    \"\"\"\n    import hashlib\n\n    if not text:\n        return None\n    return hashlib.md5(text.encode('utf-8')).hexdigest()\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/163", "solution": "```python\ndef generate_integers(a, b):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given two positive integers a and b, return the even digits between a\n    and b, in ascending order.\n    \"\"\"\n    min_val = min(a, b)\n    max_val = max(a, b)\n\n    even_integers = []\n    for i in range(min_val, max_val + 1):\n        if i % 2 == 0:\n            even_integers.append(i)\n    return even_integers\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/161", "solution": "```python\ndef solve(s):\n    \"\"\"You are given a string s.\n    if s[i] is a letter, reverse its case from lower to upper or vise versa,\n    otherwise keep it as it is.\n    If the string contains no letters, reverse the string.\n    The function should return the resulted string.\n    Examples\n    solve(\"1234\") = \"4321\"\n    solve(\"ab\") = \"AB\"\n    solve(\"#a@C\") = \"#A@c\"\n    \"\"\"\n    ALPHABET_CHARS = set('abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ')\n\n    def _reverse_char_case(char: str) -> str:\n        # Reverses the case of an alphabetic character, otherwise returns it unchanged.\n        if char.isalpha():\n            return char.swapcase()\n        return char\n\n    def _reverse_string(s: str) -> str:\n        # Reverses the entire input string.\n        return s[::-1]\n\n    def _has_letters(s: str) -> bool:\n        # Checks if the string contains any alphabetic characters.\n        for char in s:\n            if char in ALPHABET_CHARS:\n                return True\n        return False\n\n    if not _has_letters(s):\n        return _reverse_string(s)\n\n    result_chars = []\n    for char in s:\n        result_chars.append(_reverse_char_case(char))\n    return \"\".join(result_chars)\n```"}
